# Security basics

## Never commit secrets

API keys, tokens, passwords, and connection strings belong in environment
variables or a secrets manager — never in source, comments, commit messages,
or logs. If you see a hardcoded secret while editing a file, flag it and
suggest moving it to `.env` (and confirm `.env` is gitignored) rather than
propagating the pattern.

```typescript
// BAD
const client = new ApiClient({ apiKey: "sk_live_abc123..." });

// GOOD
const client = new ApiClient({ apiKey: process.env.API_KEY! });
```

## Treat all external input as untrusted

Anything from a request body, query string, uploaded file, or third-party
API response must be validated/sanitized before use in a query, shell
command, file path, or template — regardless of whether an earlier layer
"probably" already checked it.

## Least privilege by default

Default new credentials, tokens, and DB grants to the minimum scope that
works. Widening access later is a one-line change; a leaked over-privileged
credential is an incident.
